## Your rights at work

Under the Equality Act rules, **addiction to alcohol is not counted as a disability in itself**. But health conditions **caused by or linked to** drinking, such as **liver disease, depression or anxiety**, can count if they have a substantial, long-term effect on your daily life.

Many employers have policies to support staff with alcohol problems, such as time off for treatment. Asking for help early is usually better than waiting until there is a problem at work.

## Driving

You must tell the **DVLA** if you have alcohol dependence or misuse alcohol. You must not drive while unfit through drink, including the morning after. Check GOV.UK for the rules.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how your health affects you on **most days**. It does not matter what caused your condition. If alcohol dependence, or health problems linked to it, mean you need help with things like **preparing food, eating, managing treatment, managing money or getting around**, you may be entitled to PIP.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you cannot work because of your health, you may be able to get **[Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/)** or **[New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/)**. Free advisers, such as Citizens Advice, can help with benefits and debt.
